WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:51.820
PATRICK: Welcome to the Council on Foreign
Relations.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:52.900
My name is Stewart Patrick.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:57.320
I'm a senior fellow here at the Council, and
I have the privilege of directing the program

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:02.480
on International Institutions and Global Governance
and of working closely with the members of

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.280
the Council of Councils.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:11.610
I'm here this morning to kick off a very brief
overview of the Report Card on International

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:16.530
Cooperation, which is the second installment
of this annual product that we've launched.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:22.900
And then we're going to have a discussion
of-with leaders of four members of our think-tank

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:30.720
network, moderated by Marcus Mabry, who is
the managing editor of TwitterMoments.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:36.930
He'll engage the four members of this network
in a discussion about the findings that we've

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.380
had and their implications.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.290
Before we get to the main event, though, I
want to give you just a quick run-through

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:52.300
about the major components and the major findings,
the headlines of this report card.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:59.479
The Council of Councils was created in 2012,
and it brings together 26 of the world's leading

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:05.600
policy institutes to seek policy solutions
to some of the world's most pressing transnational

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.240
problems.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:12.530
Its composition-that is, of the Council of
Councils-is roughly equivalent to the Group

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.890
of 20 in terms of national representation.

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:17.530
And its rationale is similar.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:23.190
What we try to do is bridge gaps between established
and emerging powers on the requirements for

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:29.090
effective multilateral cooperation in the
21st century.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:34.430
Now, we're all aware of how urgent this goal
is.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:41.920
Wherever we look, we see transnational challenges
and problems that defy purely national solutions.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:49.420
These range from climate change to financial
volatility, nuclear proliferation, to jihadist

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:50.470
terrorism.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:55.090
One of the most poignant that we saw over
the last 12 months, obviously, is the global

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:58.000
migration crisis and refugee crisis.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.170
Now, this is something that's been going on
in other parts of the world, but it washed

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:07.220
up on Europe's shores last year and suddenly
there was lots of international attention

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.530
about it.

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:14.690
But around the world, for the first time,
more than 60 million people are displaced,

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:18.290
either as refugees or as internally displaced
people.

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:24.540
Now, all of these international problems are
occurring at a time of surging geopolitical

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:31.670
rivalry, anemic global growth, state failure
in many parts of the world, and frankly an

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:36.080
angry populism that has affected this country
and many other countries.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:44.230
Yet despite these challenges and these negative
trends, the 2016 edition of the report card

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:47.220
offers a few glimmers of hope.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:54.040
In the last year, the world took promising
signs-promising steps to mitigate and adapt

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:59.590
to climate change, most obviously in the Paris
agreements in December of this last year.

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:05.650
It also took steps in nuclear proliferation
in the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:09.560
to limit Iran's nuclear capability.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:15.930
And also in September at the U.N. General
Assembly, the world ratified or endorsed the

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:20.260
sustainable development goals, which are designed
to help organize the fight against global

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:26.810
poverty and improve human well-being around
the world through 2030.

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:33.810
Despite these bright spots, there are a number
of weaknesses, however, in terms of our approach

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.260
to global governance.

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:41.789
It's clear that international institutions
have not kept pace with fast-changing global-excuse

00:04:41.789 --> 00:04:48.690
me-with a fast-paced global context, including
the emergence of new powers and the rise of

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.090
new threats.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:56.569
Since the year 2000, the world has arguably
experienced the fastest and most dramatic

00:04:56.569 --> 00:05:01.759
redistribution of world power in modern history.

00:05:01.759 --> 00:05:05.949
Established and emerging powers often see
the world in very different ways.

00:05:05.949 --> 00:05:10.449
They have different values, and their interests
are often colliding.

00:05:10.449 --> 00:05:17.949
We see this geopolitical rivalry from Eastern
Europe to the Far East to the Middle East.

00:05:17.949 --> 00:05:26.520
And we've seen that get in the way of handling
some of the most intractable territorial and

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:29.120
other disputes in those regions.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:34.930
Meanwhile, creaky international organizations,
from the United Nations to the IMF to the

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:38.930
World Health Organization, are having a difficult
time adapting to this new world.

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:42.879
There's been no change in the composition
of the permanent membership of the Security

00:05:42.879 --> 00:05:47.830
Council since 1945, and no change in its membership
at all since the early 1960s.

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:55.120
With respect to the International Monetary
Fund, it took five years after a modest agreement

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:59.900
on the-when so-called Chairs and Shares at
the International Monetary Fund was negotiated

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:06.219
in 2010 for finally the U.S. Congress to pass
that legislation almost at the end of this

00:06:06.219 --> 00:06:08.060
last year.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:13.229
And we've seen the World Health Organization
struggle mightily to respond first to Ebola

00:06:13.229 --> 00:06:17.259
and now to try to get on top of the Zika virus.

00:06:17.259 --> 00:06:23.460
Mitigating the downside of globalization and
taking advantage of its upside requires new

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:29.400
and creative approaches to cooperation amongst
the world's most influential governments.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:34.449
But that cooperation can only occur if countries
agree on where we are and where we might be

00:06:34.449 --> 00:06:35.650
going.

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:38.919
And that's where the report card comes in.

00:06:38.919 --> 00:06:44.620
As you can see in this slide, we asked the
heads of the Council of Councils institutions

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:49.820
to assess international cooperation on 10
critical challenges.

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:56.219
They include preventing nuclear proliferation,
managing the global economy, mitigating and

00:06:56.219 --> 00:07:03.999
adapting to climate change, advancing development,
promoting global health, expanding global

00:07:03.999 --> 00:07:10.159
trade, managing cyber-governance, combating
transnational terrorism, and preventing and

00:07:10.159 --> 00:07:15.800
responding to violent conflict, both between
states and within states.

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:23.050
In each case, we asked the think-tank heads
to do three things: First, grade international

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:31.249
cooperation in 2015, both overall and in each
issue area; second, to rank these challenges

00:07:31.249 --> 00:07:37.479
on the basis of their importance; and third,
to assess opportunities for breakthrough in

00:07:37.479 --> 00:07:39.370
2016.

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:41.960
So what did we find?

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:47.919
Overall, the world made important progress
in the past year, buoyed by some of the positive

00:07:47.919 --> 00:07:49.849
developments that I described previously.

00:07:49.849 --> 00:07:56.669
The COC awarded an overall-overall efforts
a grade of "B," a full letter-grade improvement

00:07:56.669 --> 00:07:58.599
over the previous year.

00:07:58.599 --> 00:08:06.490
At the same time, there was significant variation
across issue areas.

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:12.589
In terms of grades, as in last year's edition,
the report card gave the lowest grades to

00:08:12.589 --> 00:08:18.449
violent conflict between states to efforts
to resolve issues related to transnational

00:08:18.449 --> 00:08:24.979
terrorism and to efforts to resolve or prevent
internal violent conflict.

00:08:24.979 --> 00:08:31.009
All of these are obviously intertwined in
the situation in Syria, which, as we all know,

00:08:31.009 --> 00:08:37.099
has now entered its sixth year of civil war,
with apparently no end in sight.

00:08:37.099 --> 00:08:44.430
Now, with respect to importance, you'll notice
that the lowest performers are also the biggest

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:49.590
areas that the Council identifies as the highest
priorities.

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:56.529
Also the uneven economic recovery prompted
the COC leaders-the uneven global economic

00:08:56.529 --> 00:09:04.050
recovery prompted COC leaders to put managing
the global economy right after those-that

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:07.670
cluster of conflict issues in terms of importance,
in terms of what the international community

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:10.860
needs to focus on.

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:15.700
In terms of opportunities for breakthrough,
mitigating and adapting to climate change

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:20.370
registers first here as countries begin to
implement their Paris pledges.

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:25.580
The report card was also optimistic about
advances in global trade, notwithstanding

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:29.450
the complications of trying to get agreement
on the Trans-Pacific Partnership in terms

00:09:29.450 --> 00:09:36.120
of its ratification and the Transatlantic
Trade and Investment Partnership, the negotiations

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:37.710
of which continue.

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:42.840
Once again, you'll see that conflict and terrorism
cluster together as representing the poorest

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:46.580
performers in terms of opportunities for breakthrough.

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:51.490
I'm coming to the end of my allotted time,
but I want to point out that both the hard

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:57.160
copy that you all have had distributed to
you and also the interactive Web-based version

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:02.660
contain a number of other compelling features.

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:07.170
Each issue description-that is, each description
of the 10 issue areas-includes several components.

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:12.260
One is a section we call By the Numbers, which
you'll see down below, which has revealing

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:16.140
statistics about that particular issue area.

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:21.150
Then there's more in-depth analysis that has
been written by Council on Foreign Relations

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:28.370
staff that places the grades and rankings
that you see into a broader context, an analytic

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:29.370
context.

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:33.780
We also have a graph that shows how many of
the respondents assigned each grade for each

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:35.220
particular issue area.

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:40.160
And then finally we have comments and policy
prescriptions by each of the heads of the

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:43.100
Council of Councils network.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:48.031
We also have an interactive map that displays
comments from the heads of the Council of

00:10:48.031 --> 00:10:49.490
Council member institutes.

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:56.320
And you can filter these by both country and
issue area.

00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:01.510
We encourage you to explore the Report Card
on International Cooperation, both in its

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:03.350
paper and its online form.

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:07.440
We'd love to hear back from you as to what
you think about it, where you think we might

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:11.160
be able to improve its presentation or its
analysis.

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:16.200
And we look forward to your comments as we
try to make this product as useful as possible

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:18.190
in the years ahead.

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:23.090
Before closing, I do want to thank three people
whose work was indispensable in bringing this

00:11:23.090 --> 00:11:24.620
product to fruition.

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:29.500
The first is Megan Roberts, who's my associate
director down in Washington.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:34.310
I'd also like to thank Terry (sp) Mullan,
who is coordinating our work with the Council

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:39.380
of Councils membership, and also Theresa Lou,
who has been assisting us in these-in this

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:40.940
process.

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:45.660
Now it's my great privilege to introduce Marcus
Mabry of Twitter.

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:51.110
Marcus is going to introduce his fellow panelists
and lead them in a discussion on the contemporary

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:52.640
crisis of global governance.

00:11:52.640 --> 00:12:08.980
Thank you very much.

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:25.120
(Applause.)

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:48.350
MABRY: Thank you, Stewart, for that introduction
and for that overview of the report.

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:50.440
Let me start off with some announcements.

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:52.810
Welcome to today's Council on Foreign Relations
meeting.

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:59.850
This special event is part of the 2016 Council
of Councils annual conference and is the launch

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:03.520
of the Council of Councils Report Card on
International Cooperation.

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:08.750
I'm going to start off by-we're going to talk
first up here amongst ourselves for about

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:15.680
half an hour, and then we'll toss it out to
the members and to any press to get your questions

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:19.760
asked directly to the Council of Council leaders.

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:28.540
This is obviously an incredibly august body,
both physically here in front of us and intellectually

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:35.590
and visually on the report that's in front
of you and the one that's online; 25 think

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:39.830
tanks on foreign policy, with some of the
greatest minds on our planet, tackling the

00:13:39.830 --> 00:13:42.240
issue of global governance at a critical time.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:44.860
So I think we're all very privileged to be
here this morning.

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:50.230
I'm going to very quickly do some brief introductions,
bios.

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:53.540
You have them in front of you, so I won't
go at great length.

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:58.310
But immediately to my left is Dr. Michael
Fullilove, who is the executive director of

00:13:58.310 --> 00:14:01.030
the Lowy Institute for International Policy.

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:05.690
He has been a visiting fellow in foreign policy
at the Brookings Institution and an adviser

00:14:05.690 --> 00:14:10.150
to Prime Minister Paul Keating and is the
author of "Rendezvous with Destiny: How Franklin

00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:15.690
D. Roosevelt and Five Extraordinary Men Took
America Into the War and Into the World."

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:16.690
Welcome.

00:14:16.690 --> 00:14:17.690
FULLILOVE: Thank you, Marcus.

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:23.880
MABRY: Dr. Richard Haass, many of you may
know, is the 13th-in his 13th year as president

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:27.520
of the Council on Foreign Relations, which
is extraordinary for those of us who've been

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:32.680
here this whole time-(laughs)-it happened
fast-and is the author-and is, of course-was

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:35.780
the senior Middle East adviser to President
George H.W.

00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:40.640
Bush and was a principal adviser to Secretary
of State Colin Powell under President George

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:46.330
W. Bush and also was a-the U.S. coordinator
for policy toward the future of Afghanistan,

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:48.350
amongst a whole bunch of other roles.

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:54.040
Richard is the author or editor of 12 books
on U.S. foreign policy.

00:14:54.040 --> 00:15:01.890
To his left is Professor Sook Jong Lee, who
is the president of the East Asia Institute,

00:15:01.890 --> 00:15:05.320
an independent nonprofit think tank based
on Seoul.

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:09.490
She is a professor of public administration
and holds a number of advisory positions in

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:13.690
the South Korean government, including in
the Presidential National Security Advisory

00:15:13.690 --> 00:15:19.600
Group, the Presidential Committee for Unification
Preparation, and since 2015 she has served

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:23.330
on the Steering Committee of the World Movement
for Democracy.

00:15:23.330 --> 00:15:24.330
Welcome.

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:30.090
Finally, and certainly last but certainly
not least, is General Amos Yadlin, who has

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:35.280
been the director of Tel Aviv University's
Institute for National Security Studies, Israel's

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:39.430
leading strategic think tank, since November
2011.

00:15:39.430 --> 00:15:44.380
He served for over 40 years in the Israeli
Defense Forces, nine of which he served as

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:47.390
a member of the IDF General Staff.

00:15:47.390 --> 00:15:50.880
Amongst many other appointments, he has been
the IDF's chief of defense intelligence.

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:59.100
He has accumulated about 5,000 flight hours
and a few more than 25-more than 250 combat

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:04.960
missions, including during the Yom Kippur
War and Operation Tammuz, which destroyed

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:07.720
the Osirak nuclear reactor in Iraq.

00:16:07.720 --> 00:16:10.760
As I said, a very distinguished body.

00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:11.760
Thank you all.

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:16.020
Please welcome them all for me.

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:17.270
(Applause.)

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:27.510
Now, since the meat of our body before us-obviously,
as a journalist, it is-we're usually a cynical

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:33.730
body, and so you might be-I might be forgiven
if I were to say, given the state of the world

00:16:33.730 --> 00:16:37.100
and the general pessimism that many of us
feel about it-(chuckles)-and pessimism is

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:45.529
not a light word; despair, great fear, and
dread-a "B" is a really surprising grade from

00:16:45.529 --> 00:16:46.529
your report.

00:16:46.529 --> 00:16:51.180
So I'm going to start-I'll ask Richard this
question, out of fairness to our guests: Is

00:16:51.180 --> 00:16:56.440
this a subject of grade inflation, or how
did we get this "B"?

00:16:56.440 --> 00:16:57.440
(Laughter.)

00:16:57.440 --> 00:17:00.460
HAASS: The conclusion I've reached is that
a lot of my colleagues are aiming to become

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:02.779
professors at Harvard.

00:17:02.779 --> 00:17:03.779
(Laughter.)

00:17:03.779 --> 00:17:09.530
They have understood the culture there, where
it's very hard to get below a "B."

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:15.470
So I think this is a glass-half-full sort
of take on the world.

00:17:15.470 --> 00:17:20.549
It's taking certain developments at, if you
will, face value and putting the best possible

00:17:20.549 --> 00:17:25.030
face on them, saying what was accomplished
is a good baseline and it will lead to better

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:26.039
things in the future.

00:17:26.039 --> 00:17:31.270
I think that's the way that-at the risk of
speaking for my two dozen colleagues-one would

00:17:31.270 --> 00:17:38.360
interpret something like the Paris accord
on climate change, the Iran agreement, what

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:42.570
was achieved with the Development Goals, and
trade with TPP.

00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:47.809
So all of these things were achievements on
the surface, in the sense that they got done.

00:17:47.809 --> 00:17:56.051
Where I think the glass is half-empty in every
case is that, also referring to Harvard, we

00:17:56.051 --> 00:18:00.410
used to teach at the Kennedy School that 90
percent of life was implementation, and this

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:02.250
leaves the 90 percent.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:05.070
So there's the real question about, in each
one of those things: Will the Development

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:06.540
Goals become reality?

00:18:06.540 --> 00:18:12.030
Will TPP and other trade agreements ever come
into effect?

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:17.940
In the case of climate, which I'm quite skeptical
about, you had aspirational goals at the national

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:18.960
level.

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:23.200
The collective goal, what the effect would
be on temperature, even if implemented would

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:28.830
fall way short of what the declared aims would
be.

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:30.990
But again, a lot of the goals were simply
aspirational.

00:18:30.990 --> 00:18:35.920
The Iran agreement, I expect Amos and others
will talk about it in great detail, but I

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:44.030
would-I would simply say that it left unanswered
at least as many problems as it purported

00:18:44.030 --> 00:18:45.850
to address.

00:18:45.850 --> 00:18:53.290
So I actually think the grade is quite generous,
even overly-even overly generous.

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:57.730
But I would simply, you know, put that out
there, if you will, as a-as a starting point.

00:18:57.730 --> 00:18:58.910
MABRY: Excellent.

00:18:58.910 --> 00:19:01.540
Thank you for that context.

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:07.440
Professor Lee, one of the areas in which-a
region which we have not seen, perhaps with

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:14.400
the exception perhaps of the TPP, real progress
toward this governance, even the jaw-someone

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:20.860
might call it the jawboning progress of the
agreements, inasmuch as they were agreements,

00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:22.730
is your part of the world.

00:19:22.730 --> 00:19:27.890
So in a world of increasing tension, certainly
in the South China Sea-certainly one of the

00:19:27.890 --> 00:19:35.870
areas in which the report was, indeed, pessimistic
was proliferation issues, North Korea's nuclear

00:19:35.870 --> 00:19:43.760
capabilities-what are the steps that could
be taken, as far as international governance,

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:49.180
to put us in a better position in those issues-those
regional issues in Asia?

00:19:49.180 --> 00:19:55.980
LEE: Marcus, as you have mentioned, we have
very challenging issues, especially in East

00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:56.980
Asia.

00:19:56.980 --> 00:20:04.160
We don't have a Pan-Asia governance system
to respond to these territorial issues.

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:09.880
So, therefore, and especially in South China
Sea, you know, China has drawn unilaterally

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:15.559
nine-dash line, and then there is a lot of
disputes with-especially with the Philippines

00:20:15.559 --> 00:20:20.830
in Spratly Islands and Vietnam in the Paracel
Island(s).

00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:27.030
As you know, the Philippines brought a case
to the Permanent Court of Arbitration, and

00:20:27.030 --> 00:20:34.680
is under the UNCLOS, the U.N. Convention on
Law of the Sea.

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:38.500
So the court is looking at the case.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:44.380
And as far as I know, by the end of July their
conclusion will be drawn among-from the five

00:20:44.380 --> 00:20:46.020
judges.

00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:49.150
So that would be a great blow to China.

00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:56.670
China, of course, don't respect or don't believe
the international maritime law, even though

00:20:56.670 --> 00:20:58.390
they are a member of UNCLOS.

00:20:58.390 --> 00:21:06.020
And they believe this maritime governance
was kind of dominated by the Western interests,

00:21:06.020 --> 00:21:13.910
and they like to come up with the alternative
norms to deal with these complex issues.

00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:21.920
And NPT-wise, of course, despite the successful
deal between USA and Iran, if you look at

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:25.170
North Korea, it's a terrible situation.

00:21:25.170 --> 00:21:30.900
And North Korean tested nuclear weapons 2006,
2009, and 2013, and January this year.

00:21:30.900 --> 00:21:34.220
So they detonated four times.

00:21:34.220 --> 00:21:42.080
And as you know, the U.N. Security Council
responded to this force claiming to be hydrogen

00:21:42.080 --> 00:21:54.000
detonations with a strong Resolution 2270,
covering the cargo inspections and the banning

00:21:54.000 --> 00:22:03.680
inflow of airplane fuel, and also banning
the trading the minerals such as gold and

00:22:03.680 --> 00:22:04.680
titanium.

00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:09.980
So this time it's lucky that China is more
willing to cooperate with this resolution.

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:12.500
However, there are many loopholes, you know.

00:22:12.500 --> 00:22:19.730
For example, it was (lively for the purpose
?), you know, that North Korea can export

00:22:19.730 --> 00:22:22.300
coal through Dandong city.

00:22:22.300 --> 00:22:24.720
So we'll see.

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:30.520
Because this resolution was drawn in March
2nd, I think the kind of monitoring report

00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:33.440
will come in June, as far as I know.

00:22:33.440 --> 00:22:36.700
MABRY: Do you think there's a chance that
any of these international-this international

00:22:36.700 --> 00:22:44.580
intervention, in terms of norms and laws and
constrictions, will stop North Korea?

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:45.580
It hasn't yet.

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:47.490
Will it stop North Korea's nuclear program?

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:52.610
LEE: It's very difficult because there is
no good options, you know.

00:22:52.610 --> 00:22:59.970
And this young leader, Kim Jong-un, who tested
twice after he succeeded his father, defy

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:02.809
this international law and governance.

00:23:02.809 --> 00:23:10.080
And in recent Workers Party Congress two weeks
ago, he declared that North Korea is a nuclear

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:11.080
state.

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:12.560
So I don't think he's going to give it up.

00:23:12.560 --> 00:23:17.520
But, you know, this time it is a very strong
resolution, so we have to-the international

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:25.910
society-have to work together to exercise
diplomatic pressure and in sharing information.

00:23:25.910 --> 00:23:33.920
But there is a kind of-it's very difficult
to coordinate sanctions among the major stakeholders-USA

00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:38.650
and China and Japan and South Korea.

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:42.950
MABRY: And that continues to be a weakness
of international governance, in fact.

00:23:42.950 --> 00:23:43.950
LEE: Yes.

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:49.100
MABRY: General, obviously, in your neighborhood,
we have the kind of-again, what even the report

00:23:49.100 --> 00:23:56.000
acknowledges is the most despairing situation
on the planet right now with regard to conflict,

00:23:56.000 --> 00:24:00.250
both internally displaced and trans-border
conflict.

00:24:00.250 --> 00:24:03.240
Do you see any international governance hope?

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:08.610
Because, again, it seems we've failed massively
with regards to Syria, and even in some degree

00:24:08.610 --> 00:24:09.750
with regards to ISIS.

00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:14.650
Do you see any hope for international governance
having any successes in that realm?

00:24:14.650 --> 00:24:19.020
Or is all lost, and must we appeal to something
other than transnational institutions?

00:24:19.020 --> 00:24:26.870
YADLIN: Yeah, I want to first refer to the
report optimism, because there are a saying

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:29.990
that a pessimist is an optimist with experience.

00:24:29.990 --> 00:24:30.990
(Laughter.)

00:24:30.990 --> 00:24:37.210
So you see that the head of the think tanks
are quite optimist even though they are very

00:24:37.210 --> 00:24:44.210
experienced, or they gave the job to their
young research assistant.

00:24:44.210 --> 00:24:45.450
(Laughter.)

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:55.090
Albert Einstein was asked by one of his students
about the coming examination.

00:24:55.090 --> 00:25:00.680
And the student asked, are the questions going
to be the same like last year?

00:25:00.680 --> 00:25:07.220
And Einstein replied that the question will
be the same question, but the answer is going

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:08.950
to be different this year.

00:25:08.950 --> 00:25:09.950
(Laughter.)

00:25:09.950 --> 00:25:13.760
Which means the world is changing, and changing
fast.

00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:22.720
And if you take my region, there are like
five states that, when we went to high school,

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:29.080
they were states-Iraq, Syria, Libya, Yemen,
Lebanon-not anymore.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:38.429
There are new states that we never recognized,
but they are there on the ground: Kurdistan,

00:25:38.429 --> 00:25:41.929
Hamas-stan, the Islamic State.

00:25:41.929 --> 00:25:43.250
So it's a different world.

00:25:43.250 --> 00:25:52.210
And sometimes we are judging it with rules
and norms and international governance that

00:25:52.210 --> 00:25:54.830
belong to a different period.

00:25:54.830 --> 00:26:04.630
I think the grade here is reflecting a very
short-range looking at the world.

00:26:04.630 --> 00:26:12.390
And maybe for 2016 it's the right view, but
my concern is for the long run.

00:26:12.390 --> 00:26:15.510
And I think think tanks should look at the
long run.

00:26:15.510 --> 00:26:25.179
And there is a methodology issue here, that
the most important problems are on the lowest

00:26:25.179 --> 00:26:27.549
grade.

00:26:27.549 --> 00:26:36.320
So somebody has to do the balance and to give
it some-in the final grade, how come the most

00:26:36.320 --> 00:26:42.460
important issue get the lowest grade, and
they are the most difficult to deal with?

00:26:42.460 --> 00:26:48.070
This is very important finding of the report
and need to change the final score.

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:57.420
So, on proliferation, I think Iran is a wonderful
example, because my grade to the Iranian deal

00:26:57.420 --> 00:27:01.940
was not "F" like my prime minister, which
says this is a new Holocaust.

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:07.960
It is not a new Holocaust because Israel is
very strong and Israel can cope even with

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:15.510
nuclear Iran, even though nuclear Iran is
a very, very grave and bleak development.

00:27:15.510 --> 00:27:20.810
It is not a diplomatic achievement that will
bring another Nobel Prize.

00:27:20.810 --> 00:27:23.530
No, it's in the middle.

00:27:23.530 --> 00:27:31.480
In the short run, it's "B." It's "B" because
Iran is rolled back a year.

00:27:31.480 --> 00:27:37.310
And if-and this is a big "if"-if they will
keep the agreement, they will stay there for

00:27:37.310 --> 00:27:39.910
10, 12, 15 years.

00:27:39.910 --> 00:27:44.480
Depends on what area of the-of the agreement.

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:47.510
However, it is not "A-minus."

00:27:47.510 --> 00:27:50.270
"A-minus" was three years.

00:27:50.270 --> 00:27:54.890
"A" was, you know, no nuclear capabilities
in Iran.

00:27:54.890 --> 00:27:58.790
So still very close to the bomb upon this
issue.

00:27:58.790 --> 00:28:02.750
But the "F" is in the long run.

00:28:02.750 --> 00:28:10.200
The "F" is in the long run, because in the
long run this agreement gave Iran a legitimacy

00:28:10.200 --> 00:28:18.260
to have an unlimited nuclear program with
200,000 centrifuges if they wish, 30 nuclear

00:28:18.260 --> 00:28:21.300
reactor(s) if they wish.

00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:23.590
MABRY: How do you define the long run?

00:28:23.590 --> 00:28:25.700
What-
YADLIN: Ten to 15 years.

00:28:25.700 --> 00:28:26.700
This is the agreement.

00:28:26.700 --> 00:28:27.700
HAASS: When the ceilings expire.

00:28:27.700 --> 00:28:34.360
YADLIN: And if we are at that time with the
same regime that called to the elimination

00:28:34.360 --> 00:28:40.920
of my country, this is a big problem for the
world security and safety.

00:28:40.920 --> 00:28:46.470
And then you take North Korea, that basically
signed the same agreement in the '90s and

00:28:46.470 --> 00:28:49.559
violated after a couple of years.

00:28:49.559 --> 00:28:57.070
So I think the main challenge for us is not
to argue about how good is the deal, it's

00:28:57.070 --> 00:28:59.240
to look into the future 10 years.

00:28:59.240 --> 00:29:02.430
And here are very shortly three scenarios.

00:29:02.430 --> 00:29:09.510
One, optimistic: the Iranians are changing,
the young generation, the Facebook generation,

00:29:09.510 --> 00:29:10.740
the Twitter generation-
MABRY: Thank you.

00:29:10.740 --> 00:29:11.740
(Laughter.)

00:29:11.740 --> 00:29:15.299
YADLIN: -is coming forward and taking over
from the mullahs.

00:29:15.299 --> 00:29:20.970
OK, this is an optimistic scenario: the Iranians
become Norwegians, wonderful.

00:29:20.970 --> 00:29:21.990
(Laughter.)

00:29:21.990 --> 00:29:24.050
Percentage, Richard?

00:29:24.050 --> 00:29:26.010
HAASS: Approximately 5 percent.

00:29:26.010 --> 00:29:27.690
YADLIN: Five percent.

00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:30.179
Amazing, my number as well.

00:29:30.179 --> 00:29:31.179
(Laughter.)

00:29:31.179 --> 00:29:35.390
Even though many Americans say, "General,
0.5 (percent)."

00:29:35.390 --> 00:29:38.100
But, second, North Korean scenario.

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:44.140
After three years, four years, they got all
the money, they built a better military, and

00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:46.440
they violate the agreement.

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:48.140
So North Korea scenario.

00:29:48.140 --> 00:29:53.860
And there is even more dangerous one, which
is the Iranian scenario: strategic patience,

00:29:53.860 --> 00:29:59.390
and within 10 to 15 years-and this is not
General Yadlin; this is President Obama saying-zero

00:29:59.390 --> 00:30:02.100
distance from the bomb.

00:30:02.100 --> 00:30:05.120
So it couldn't be an "A-minus."

00:30:05.120 --> 00:30:13.210
MABRY: On that question, and I have to ask
you as such an informed source, is it not

00:30:13.210 --> 00:30:16.360
inevitable, though, that Iran will be a nuclear
state?

00:30:16.360 --> 00:30:21.600
And therefore, is not the "F" inevitable?

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:28.880
YADLIN: It is-it is possible to stop them,
as some other countries were stopped.

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:31.549
Iraq, you mentioned the case.

00:30:31.549 --> 00:30:36.370
According to the international media, Syria
2007.

00:30:36.370 --> 00:30:38.060
Think about Syria today.

00:30:38.060 --> 00:30:45.160
Al Kibar, where the nuclear- the North Korean
nuclear reactor, which has only one use, for

00:30:45.160 --> 00:30:53.980
military war, is in the hands of-this is a
territory now occupied by ISIS.

00:30:53.980 --> 00:30:55.720
Think about it.

00:30:55.720 --> 00:30:59.470
And ISIS hasn't attacked Israel.

00:30:59.470 --> 00:31:04.160
London, Brussels, Turkey, Syria, Iraq.

00:31:04.160 --> 00:31:08.980
So I think Iran should not have a nuclear
program.

00:31:08.980 --> 00:31:17.880
And they can be stopped if the countries that-by
the way, this is the only point that President

00:31:17.880 --> 00:31:25.150
Obama and Prime Minister Netanyahu agrees-only
point-which say that Iran will not have the

00:31:25.150 --> 00:31:26.690
nuclear bomb.

00:31:26.690 --> 00:31:35.710
So let's hope that the two gentlemen will,
in case of the two bleak scenarios, not the

00:31:35.710 --> 00:31:41.600
optimistic one, will do what they have to
do in the next-if Iran will violate the agreement.

00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:46.740
HAASS: Marcus, if I could say one thing, it
wasn't inevitable that North Korea reach this

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:47.740
point.

00:31:47.740 --> 00:31:52.200
The United States and others made decisions
in the early 1990s not to act when there were

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:55.760
military options to do it.

00:31:55.760 --> 00:31:57.350
Now we've got to live with the consequences.

00:31:57.350 --> 00:32:00.640
And there are still options if the United
States and others want to put them on the

00:32:00.640 --> 00:32:07.260
table to force North Korea out of the nuclear
business, particularly if China will cooperate.

00:32:07.260 --> 00:32:09.200
MABRY: Military options, you're talking about.

00:32:09.200 --> 00:32:12.370
HAASS: Or even short of military options,
if China will cooperate.

00:32:12.370 --> 00:32:16.900
If China were to ramp up sanctions against
North Korea, I don't think the country could

00:32:16.900 --> 00:32:19.940
survive for more than a matter of weeks.

00:32:19.940 --> 00:32:22.150
So there are options against North Korea.

00:32:22.150 --> 00:32:23.770
There are options against Iran.

00:32:23.770 --> 00:32:26.850
There were historically options against others.

00:32:26.850 --> 00:32:29.700
Nuclear proliferation is not inevitable.

00:32:29.700 --> 00:32:33.870
What it is is, yes, you've got countries that
are determined to go down that path, but the

00:32:33.870 --> 00:32:38.440
United States and other countries have made
political calculations, weighing their opposition

00:32:38.440 --> 00:32:41.669
to nonproliferation against other interests.

00:32:41.669 --> 00:32:47.240
And we have often been prepared to essentially
life with certain outcomes because we had

00:32:47.240 --> 00:32:52.950
competing interests-whether it was cooperating
with Pakistan in certain contexts or what

00:32:52.950 --> 00:32:53.950
have you.

00:32:53.950 --> 00:32:56.990
So there's very little that's inevitable.

00:32:56.990 --> 00:33:02.070
Most of what happens is the result of a conscious
or in some cases unconscious choices.

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:08.140
MABRY: And as you know better than probably
anyone in the room, the calculation there,

00:33:08.140 --> 00:33:11.789
the means interest calculation-the interest
calculation-the competing interest calculations,

00:33:11.789 --> 00:33:14.210
are sometimes political and sometimes economic.

00:33:14.210 --> 00:33:18.690
Sometimes it's a loss of life that is not
considered to be politically viable.

00:33:18.690 --> 00:33:20.110
HAASS: These are tough choices.

00:33:20.110 --> 00:33:22.440
I don't mean to sit here and be glib.

00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:26.970
And in the early 1990s, for example, the United
States, when North Korea's nuclear program

00:33:26.970 --> 00:33:32.060
consisted of rods and cooling ponds, before
they moved them there was a major debate in

00:33:32.060 --> 00:33:37.210
the Clinton administration and beyond whether
that was the moment to attack North Korea.

00:33:37.210 --> 00:33:40.500
Now, it could have been done and we could
have stopped their nuclear program.

00:33:40.500 --> 00:33:42.620
North Korea could have retaliated in many
ways.

00:33:42.620 --> 00:33:44.280
There probably would have been a war on the
peninsula.

00:33:44.280 --> 00:33:49.010
It would have been extreme consequential for
South Korea, for Japan and the United States

00:33:49.010 --> 00:33:51.440
given our treaty-
MABRY: How many losses in South Korea?

00:33:51.440 --> 00:33:52.440
HAASS: Pardon me?

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:54.280
MABRY: How many losses-
HAASS: Well, we don't know.

00:33:54.280 --> 00:33:59.351
Again, you can play-we can't say for certain
how it would have played out militarily, how

00:33:59.351 --> 00:34:00.970
long it would have gone on, and so forth.

00:34:00.970 --> 00:34:05.919
But obviously given where Seoul is, given
North Korea's conventional military might,

00:34:05.919 --> 00:34:08.419
you have to assume it would have been consequential.

00:34:08.419 --> 00:34:12.559
So I'm not saying that the-I'm not saying
the alternatives were cheap or easy.

00:34:12.559 --> 00:34:13.609
Again, I'm not being glib.

00:34:13.609 --> 00:34:18.690
I'm simply saying that choices not to act
against proliferation, for legitimate reasons,

00:34:18.690 --> 00:34:20.310
had tremendous consequences.

00:34:20.310 --> 00:34:21.809
And we're living with them today.

00:34:21.809 --> 00:34:27.030
And quite honestly, I would think one of the
next great foreign policy crises, that is

00:34:27.030 --> 00:34:32.270
quite possible the next occupant of the Oval
Office will face, is what to do if and when

00:34:32.270 --> 00:34:36.909
the director of national intelligence waltzes
into the Oval Office one morning and says:

00:34:36.909 --> 00:34:40.830
North Korea can now put a number of small
nuclear warheads on missiles that can reach

00:34:40.830 --> 00:34:41.830
California.

00:34:41.830 --> 00:34:44.139
Are we prepared to tolerate this?

00:34:44.139 --> 00:34:46.530
If not, what are you prepared to do, Mr. President?

00:34:46.530 --> 00:34:49.310
Here is a set of really unattractive options.

00:34:49.310 --> 00:34:52.860
And that day is likely to come about somewhere
within the next five years.

00:34:52.860 --> 00:34:54.170
LEE: Marcus, can I add?

00:34:54.170 --> 00:34:55.170
MABRY: Yes.

00:34:55.170 --> 00:35:01.780
LEE: You know, President Obama has been taking
strategic patience to North Korea with the

00:35:01.780 --> 00:35:06.369
availability of missile and lightening the
nuclear warhead.

00:35:06.369 --> 00:35:12.090
And now there's serious worry about North
Korea's nuclear threat, right?

00:35:12.090 --> 00:35:14.460
So I think that they will engage.

00:35:14.460 --> 00:35:19.410
However, whatever mode of engagement will
be-and it's very offensive for South Korea

00:35:19.410 --> 00:35:24.180
to calculate how many South Koreans can die
if they take military options.

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:31.651
So I would strongly oppose any military strike
because in the Korean south is too close to

00:35:31.651 --> 00:35:32.651
Pyongyang.

00:35:32.651 --> 00:35:38.800
So any damages are, you know, sacrificing
our own citizens is not negotiable.

00:35:38.800 --> 00:35:45.690
MABRY: It's excellent to have the very hard
choices to talk about here on the stage this

00:35:45.690 --> 00:35:46.690
morning.

00:35:46.690 --> 00:35:49.200
Dr. Fullilove, thank you for your patience,
and I turn to you for-

00:35:49.200 --> 00:35:50.200
FULLILOVE: Strategic patience.

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:51.200
(Laughter.)

00:35:51.200 --> 00:35:52.200
MABRY: Exactly.

00:35:52.200 --> 00:35:53.200
Exactly.

00:35:53.200 --> 00:35:54.260
I do like that strategic patience thing from
now on.

00:35:54.260 --> 00:35:59.619
I'm going to use that, with regards to my
six-year old twins, because I have no strategic

00:35:59.619 --> 00:36:00.619
patience.

00:36:00.619 --> 00:36:01.760
I'm going to have to develop some.

00:36:01.760 --> 00:36:06.830
Dr. Fullilove, in this context right now,
we've heard about national interest.

00:36:06.830 --> 00:36:10.970
And we've been talking for a few minutes,
you know, competing national interests among

00:36:10.970 --> 00:36:13.220
the closest allies.

00:36:13.220 --> 00:36:20.571
In this world, what hope is there-I turn that
same question-for transnational, international

00:36:20.571 --> 00:36:21.571
governance?

00:36:21.571 --> 00:36:26.050
I mean, on a bilateral situation we can't
agree.

00:36:26.050 --> 00:36:32.350
So internally the states are falling apart
that barely ever existed and new states are

00:36:32.350 --> 00:36:37.320
coming into being which are not recognized
as states, or as actors even.

00:36:37.320 --> 00:36:39.780
What hope is there for any of this governance
issue?

00:36:39.780 --> 00:36:43.150
FULLILOVE: Well, let me make a few introductory
comments, if I can.

00:36:43.150 --> 00:36:48.920
First of all, can I thank Richard and his
colleagues at CFR for hosting us at the Council

00:36:48.920 --> 00:36:50.300
of Councils.

00:36:50.300 --> 00:36:53.849
And can I commend Richard for his vision in
establishing the Council of Councils.

00:36:53.849 --> 00:37:00.180
I know it sounds a bit like the Illuminati-(laughter)-or
I'm always reminded of-you remember the commission

00:37:00.180 --> 00:37:04.570
in the Godfather movies, where the heads of
the different families come together to solve

00:37:04.570 --> 00:37:05.570
their problems?

00:37:05.570 --> 00:37:07.109
HAASS: You really have to go down this path?

00:37:07.109 --> 00:37:08.109
(Laughter.)

00:37:08.109 --> 00:37:10.230
MABRY: What does that make Richard?

00:37:10.230 --> 00:37:11.230
Who does that make Richard?

00:37:11.230 --> 00:37:12.230
HAASS: My Twitter feed now will-(laughter)-
FULLILOVE: I'm trying to help Marcus with

00:37:12.230 --> 00:37:13.230
company.

00:37:13.230 --> 00:37:14.230
(Laughter.)

00:37:14.230 --> 00:37:15.230
MABRY: And we appreciate it.

00:37:15.230 --> 00:37:17.440
FULLILOVE: Yeah.

00:37:17.440 --> 00:37:23.180
No, but seriously, I think the Council of
Councils is a very powerful organization with

00:37:23.180 --> 00:37:24.640
a great deal of potential.

00:37:24.640 --> 00:37:29.320
And I find it incredibly valuable to get together
every year with my counterparts.

00:37:29.320 --> 00:37:33.050
And I want to thank the Stewart in particular,
and his team, for putting together this report

00:37:33.050 --> 00:37:34.050
card.

00:37:34.050 --> 00:37:35.640
And I think we've had a great conversation.

00:37:35.640 --> 00:37:39.960
Everybody's going to disagree with the report
card in some way, and I'm going to pile on.

00:37:39.960 --> 00:37:43.310
But it's getting us talking about what the
issues are.

00:37:43.310 --> 00:37:45.690
I'm a historian by training.

00:37:45.690 --> 00:37:50.290
And so why these grades seem odd to me is
that, as Amos says, when you look at the long

00:37:50.290 --> 00:37:53.400
view you end up much more pessimistic about
it.

00:37:53.400 --> 00:37:58.710
You remember when Dean Acheson wrote his memoir
of his time as President Truman's secretary

00:37:58.710 --> 00:38:01.870
of state, he called it present at the creation.

00:38:01.870 --> 00:38:06.020
And indeed, Acheson's generation did create
the post-war world.

00:38:06.020 --> 00:38:08.680
They rescued Europe from financial ruin.

00:38:08.680 --> 00:38:12.200
They created the institutions that we're talking
about.

00:38:12.200 --> 00:38:17.560
But now, 70 years later, Acheson's creation
is beset on all sides.

00:38:17.560 --> 00:38:21.550
The liberal international order is less liberal,
less international, less orderly.

00:38:21.550 --> 00:38:27.250
The United States is inching back from the
world, or has inched back in the last few

00:38:27.250 --> 00:38:28.250
years.

00:38:28.250 --> 00:38:32.980
Other challenges are inching forward, which
are making resolution to some of these questions

00:38:32.980 --> 00:38:34.950
like North Korea more difficult.

00:38:34.950 --> 00:38:41.960
The Middle East, as Amos said, is-the old
state system is a state of collapse.

00:38:41.960 --> 00:38:43.930
The planet is heating up.

00:38:43.930 --> 00:38:47.420
There are more refugees-we haven't touched
on this-there are more refugees and asylum

00:38:47.420 --> 00:38:51.970
seekers than at any time since the end of
the Second World War.

00:38:51.970 --> 00:38:58.500
So it feels to me more like we are present
at the destruction of an order that was created

00:38:58.500 --> 00:39:03.830
at the Second World War, and one that has
been remarkably congenial to the interests

00:39:03.830 --> 00:39:06.870
of the countries represented on the stage.

00:39:06.870 --> 00:39:09.180
So I think it's a bracing moment.

00:39:09.180 --> 00:39:15.870
How do you square that historical perspective
with the grades, apart from the fact that

00:39:15.870 --> 00:39:19.780
my colleagues on the Council of Council are
optimists?

00:39:19.780 --> 00:39:22.090
I think in a couple of ways.

00:39:22.090 --> 00:39:28.391
One is that these grades I think are a reflection
of current performance, in a way, relative

00:39:28.391 --> 00:39:30.880
to last year's performance.

00:39:30.880 --> 00:39:34.720
I think there's also a sense, as Amos says,
that the rankings are as important as the

00:39:34.720 --> 00:39:35.970
grades.

00:39:35.970 --> 00:39:41.990
It's striking to me that these three conflict
issues are ranked by everybody as the top

00:39:41.990 --> 00:39:48.990
global challenges, the bottom of current performance,
and the bottom of the rankings in terms of

00:39:48.990 --> 00:39:50.870
opportunities in the future.

00:39:50.870 --> 00:39:57.180
But you asked me about where-what we can do
differently and what is the hope for global

00:39:57.180 --> 00:39:58.250
governance.

00:39:58.250 --> 00:40:04.350
Well, it's an impolite thing to say at a conference
on global governance, but to me the role of

00:40:04.350 --> 00:40:10.080
the United States and the U.S. allies is much
more important in preserving international

00:40:10.080 --> 00:40:13.790
order than the United Nations or other institutions.

00:40:13.790 --> 00:40:16.420
That's not to say those institutions aren't
important.

00:40:16.420 --> 00:40:17.430
They are.

00:40:17.430 --> 00:40:24.480
But in terms of the underlying power dynamics
of how the world works, I think these big

00:40:24.480 --> 00:40:28.210
nations led by the United States is the most
important issue.

00:40:28.210 --> 00:40:31.160
And so to me, what can we all do?

00:40:31.160 --> 00:40:37.500
Well, we would like to see a United States
that is engaged in the world, that steps back

00:40:37.500 --> 00:40:41.030
into the world, that leads.

00:40:41.030 --> 00:40:48.251
I would like to see a Western bloc that picks
up, that recovers its mojo in a way, that

00:40:48.251 --> 00:40:52.890
focuses on its external obligations, as well
as its internal debacles.

00:40:52.890 --> 00:40:59.230
You know, I sometimes feel at the moment that
we in the West are a bit like the priest and

00:40:59.230 --> 00:41:01.940
the Levite in the parable of the good Samaritan.

00:41:01.940 --> 00:41:09.000
We come across something unpleasant-an unpleasant
scene, like a neighbor being set upon by an

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:13.310
aggressor, as in the case of Crimea, and we
prefer to pass by on the other side.

00:41:13.310 --> 00:41:15.040
And that's last year's news.

00:41:15.040 --> 00:41:21.030
We're not talking about Crimea anymore, even
though when Russia invaded Crimea it breached

00:41:21.030 --> 00:41:26.670
the central tenet of the state system on which
our international system has been based for

00:41:26.670 --> 00:41:27.670
70 years.

00:41:27.670 --> 00:41:32.849
So I'm an Australian, therefore I'm more on
the pessimistic side naturally than the optimistic

00:41:32.849 --> 00:41:33.849
side.

00:41:33.849 --> 00:41:36.390
I'm an historian, so I'm a bit concerned about
where we are.

00:41:36.390 --> 00:41:42.599
But in terms of a positive vision, it's not
so much, I don't think, about tinkering with

00:41:42.599 --> 00:41:44.240
institutions, although that's important.

00:41:44.240 --> 00:41:48.880
It's about first of all the United States
and how it sees its role in the world, and

00:41:48.880 --> 00:41:54.490
secondly how those of us who are allied with
the United States and friendly with the United

00:41:54.490 --> 00:42:00.599
States, and like-minded, how we feel we can
contribute to solving some of these awful,

00:42:00.599 --> 00:42:01.599
bloody challenges.

00:42:01.599 --> 00:42:05.860
MABRY: Well, that being the case, and given
your own role and history as a scholar of

00:42:05.860 --> 00:42:09.920
the United States, a student of the United
States' history and its politics, do you have

00:42:09.920 --> 00:42:20.210
any cause for optimism, given what's happening
in the United States?

00:42:20.210 --> 00:42:22.470
(Laughter.)

00:42:22.470 --> 00:42:32.150
FULLILOVE: Look-(laughter)-look, I would say
this, that there is obviously a circus atmosphere

00:42:32.150 --> 00:42:34.470
in your politics at the moment.

00:42:34.470 --> 00:42:38.720
Like kind of a Coney Island sort of feel.

00:42:38.720 --> 00:42:43.900
And I'll be honest, there's something to that
in my own country's politics at the moment.

00:42:43.900 --> 00:42:48.400
And it's one of the things we were discussing
at breakfast that sort of underline the drooping

00:42:48.400 --> 00:42:53.400
confidence in the West is that in so many
of our countries there is a disaffection with

00:42:53.400 --> 00:43:02.089
elites, there is a growing populism, there's
a lack of-there's a lack of patience in the

00:43:02.089 --> 00:43:03.089
public.

00:43:03.089 --> 00:43:08.339
I mean, in my own country, for example, we
had 25 years where there were only two governments.

00:43:08.339 --> 00:43:15.650
So in other words, governments could consistently
articulate a vision, win reelection, and then

00:43:15.650 --> 00:43:17.940
prosecute those changes.

00:43:17.940 --> 00:43:21.089
And so we had three prime ministers in 25
years.

00:43:21.089 --> 00:43:24.180
And in the last six or seven years, we've
had five prime ministers.

00:43:24.180 --> 00:43:26.690
We're the Italy of the Pacific.

00:43:26.690 --> 00:43:29.440
Actually, Italy is much more stable now than
we are.

00:43:29.440 --> 00:43:30.440
(Laughter.)

00:43:30.440 --> 00:43:34.430
So I'm not really in a position to lecture
you about your politics.

00:43:34.430 --> 00:43:42.580
But I guess to me, it's-I mean, we can criticize
individual politicians, like Mr. Trump.

00:43:42.580 --> 00:43:49.950
But I think actually we need to look at ourselves
as publics and say: When did we lose the patience?

00:43:49.950 --> 00:43:59.670
When did we lose the ability to make long-term
decisions, to see the long term, not to judge

00:43:59.670 --> 00:44:05.270
in terms of our-not to give way to our own
prejudices and our own instincts, but to sort

00:44:05.270 --> 00:44:07.620
of apply some critical reason to it?

00:44:07.620 --> 00:44:09.450
I think Australians need to do that.

00:44:09.450 --> 00:44:15.480
And I'd respectfully submit that if Americans
do that, then I think there is some room for

00:44:15.480 --> 00:44:22.010
optimism, because I think the general perception
is that although President Obama has been

00:44:22.010 --> 00:44:27.480
a terrific president and the right president
to come after President Bush, that perhaps

00:44:27.480 --> 00:44:32.839
America has overlearned the lessons of the
Iraq War, and inched back a little bit more

00:44:32.839 --> 00:44:34.960
from the world than it should have.

00:44:34.960 --> 00:44:37.080
And it's time for the United States to step
back in.

00:44:37.080 --> 00:44:39.010
So fingers crossed.

00:44:39.010 --> 00:44:40.820
MABRY: Do you want to?

00:44:40.820 --> 00:44:41.910
HAASS: Two reactions.

00:44:41.910 --> 00:44:42.910
One on the U.S.

00:44:42.910 --> 00:44:47.650
I thought the most interesting thing in the
last few months was the Pew poll that came

00:44:47.650 --> 00:44:48.780
out recently.

00:44:48.780 --> 00:44:54.160
And what it essentially said is that a majority
of Americans think that we're spending too

00:44:54.160 --> 00:44:59.980
much, as a country, of our resources and attention
on the world and much more concern with things

00:44:59.980 --> 00:45:00.980
going on here.

00:45:00.980 --> 00:45:04.110
I think the obvious frustration with that
is they don't see the connection between what's

00:45:04.110 --> 00:45:07.320
going on out there and what's going on here.

00:45:07.320 --> 00:45:10.330
But I actually think it is reflective of where
the country is.

00:45:10.330 --> 00:45:15.780
There is a pushback against what are the perceived
effects of globalization, against trade.

00:45:15.780 --> 00:45:20.010
There's still the intervention fatigue after
Iraq and Afghanistan.

00:45:20.010 --> 00:45:23.000
So I think it goes far beyond any personality
or candidate.

00:45:23.000 --> 00:45:26.950
I actually think this is something of the
zeitgeist of the country, and I think it's

00:45:26.950 --> 00:45:33.020
a reality that any person who, you know, comes
into office this, you know, and takes the

00:45:33.020 --> 00:45:38.190
oath of office in January is going to have
to-going to have to contend with.

00:45:38.190 --> 00:45:43.839
So I think Michael's going to be a little
bit disappointed if he expects to see a tremendous

00:45:43.839 --> 00:45:44.839
revival.

00:45:44.839 --> 00:45:47.080
There may be some inching back, and I think
that's probably likely.

00:45:47.080 --> 00:45:50.440
But I don't think you're going to see anything
fundamental.

00:45:50.440 --> 00:45:54.950
Secondly, I think, in terms of the world-and
it's implicit in what I think you've heard

00:45:54.950 --> 00:46:00.310
up here-is-I think the quickest way I could
summarize it is that there's few phrases in

00:46:00.310 --> 00:46:04.520
our business that are invoked more often than
"international community."

00:46:04.520 --> 00:46:10.900
And I think part of the underlying reality
is there is very little international community.

00:46:10.900 --> 00:46:13.849
There isn't the consensus about what ought
to be the rules.

00:46:13.849 --> 00:46:17.050
There isn't a willingness to act on their
behalf.

00:46:17.050 --> 00:46:20.401
So the phrase is invoked as though it were
a reality.

00:46:20.401 --> 00:46:23.490
And while there's elements of it in certain
areas, and we see it in the report-there might

00:46:23.490 --> 00:46:28.920
be elements of it in the economic, or elements
of it particularly in principle in certain

00:46:28.920 --> 00:46:32.820
areas-what there are are very few elements
of it in practice.

00:46:32.820 --> 00:46:36.280
And indeed, in some cases there's downright
opponents of it.

00:46:36.280 --> 00:46:40.441
So you can't speak of an international community
in the Middle East when you have countries

00:46:40.441 --> 00:46:43.760
like Iran or various groups pursuing their
goals.

00:46:43.760 --> 00:46:48.090
Or you can't speak of an international community
when it comes to the Asia-Pacific, given some

00:46:48.090 --> 00:46:53.740
of the behaviors, obviously of North Korea
but also, say, China, or what Russia's doing

00:46:53.740 --> 00:46:56.780
in Europe, or in cyberspace.

00:46:56.780 --> 00:47:00.950
There isn't a community of thought about what
the role of governments ought to be in setting

00:47:00.950 --> 00:47:03.570
the norms, much less what the norms ought
to be.

00:47:03.570 --> 00:47:09.560
So I think that this report is taking place
in a-in a context in which the realities are

00:47:09.560 --> 00:47:13.130
in many cases far, far south of what the hopes
are.

00:47:13.130 --> 00:47:15.670
And I think, again, that's what we're capturing
in the report.

00:47:15.670 --> 00:47:17.070
The report is upbeat.

00:47:17.070 --> 00:47:21.150
I think the reality is considerably less rosy
than that.

00:47:21.150 --> 00:47:24.869
MABRY: Oftentimes in the American polity we
talk about this as a challenge of leadership,

00:47:24.869 --> 00:47:28.280
and if the right leader came along, they could
make it all change.

00:47:28.280 --> 00:47:29.280
Is that reasonable?

00:47:29.280 --> 00:47:32.110
HAASS: I think that's exaggerated.

00:47:32.110 --> 00:47:38.010
If I could ban certain things in writing,
one would be the last paragraph of virtually

00:47:38.010 --> 00:47:42.890
half the articles and books I read: "but if
there's the adequate amount of political will

00:47:42.890 --> 00:47:45.349
and leadership, all will be solved."

00:47:45.349 --> 00:47:48.630
Well, look, leadership also depends upon context.

00:47:48.630 --> 00:47:50.790
It depends on followership.

00:47:50.790 --> 00:47:53.470
So leadership can basically point in certain
directions.

00:47:53.470 --> 00:47:55.700
But again, leaders can't choose their inbox.

00:47:55.700 --> 00:47:57.650
They can't choose their context.

00:47:57.650 --> 00:47:58.650
They can shape it.

00:47:58.650 --> 00:48:00.090
They can have a difference on it.

00:48:00.090 --> 00:48:02.000
You know, human agency can matter.

00:48:02.000 --> 00:48:03.920
But it's not simply about leaders.

00:48:03.920 --> 00:48:08.860
I think that's-and that's true within the
United States, and it's true within the world

00:48:08.860 --> 00:48:11.530
given the role of the United States.

00:48:11.530 --> 00:48:18.211
It's more than simply of some abstract measure
of leadership, as though that were that you-if

00:48:18.211 --> 00:48:21.600
you only had the leadership, solutions would
follow.

00:48:21.600 --> 00:48:23.050
If only it were so.

00:48:23.050 --> 00:48:27.600
MABRY: At this time, I would like to invite
members to join our conversation with their

00:48:27.600 --> 00:48:28.600
questions.

00:48:28.600 --> 00:48:32.920
A reminder that this meeting is on the record,
and please wait for the microphone.

00:48:32.920 --> 00:48:34.589
Speak directly into it.

00:48:34.589 --> 00:48:35.760
Please stand.

00:48:35.760 --> 00:48:37.470
State you name and affiliation.

00:48:37.470 --> 00:48:41.820
And please limit yourself to one question,
and keep it concise to allow as many members

00:48:41.820 --> 00:48:44.599
as possible to speak.

00:48:44.599 --> 00:48:47.650
OK, so right here in the first table.

00:48:47.650 --> 00:48:48.650
Right here.

00:48:48.650 --> 00:48:51.660
Here ma'am, here's the mic.

00:48:51.660 --> 00:48:54.630
Q: Nan Keohane, Princeton University.

00:48:54.630 --> 00:48:58.720
I was thinking about your grading system,
and I wanted to ask a question about the conditions

00:48:58.720 --> 00:49:00.730
for grade improvement.

00:49:00.730 --> 00:49:05.470
In climate change, you have a move from a
"C-plus" to an "A" in one year.

00:49:05.470 --> 00:49:08.410
As a professor, I regard that as amazing.

00:49:08.410 --> 00:49:12.770
And I wanted to ask you all, whoever would
like to answer, whether there are any lessons

00:49:12.770 --> 00:49:17.660
to be learned from the climate-change area
for other problems that we face?

00:49:17.660 --> 00:49:19.280
I know it's mostly Paris.

00:49:19.280 --> 00:49:23.960
We can argue about how effective Paris is
or was, but it's clearly significant for bringing

00:49:23.960 --> 00:49:25.190
people together.

00:49:25.190 --> 00:49:26.580
What can we learn from Paris?

00:49:26.580 --> 00:49:28.830
MABRY: Are there lessons from the other areas?

00:49:28.830 --> 00:49:29.830
Richard?

00:49:29.830 --> 00:49:31.970
HAASS: I think there is a big lesson.

00:49:31.970 --> 00:49:36.140
The reason you had some-at least the appearance,
and perhaps the reality of progress of Paris

00:49:36.140 --> 00:49:41.320
this year was in no small part because Paris
represented a fundamental departure from previous

00:49:41.320 --> 00:49:44.630
efforts to come up with a global agreement
on climate change.

00:49:44.630 --> 00:49:51.161
So, gone was the idea of a top-down market,
you know, approach-so, cap and trade, where

00:49:51.161 --> 00:49:54.860
everyone was assigned market shares with some
kind of a trading system.

00:49:54.860 --> 00:49:57.440
Gone was the idea of creating a carbon tax.

00:49:57.440 --> 00:50:04.190
It may make the most sense in purely economic
assessments, but it simply can't be negotiated.

00:50:04.190 --> 00:50:05.619
So, what did Paris do?

00:50:05.619 --> 00:50:07.920
Paris was actually a big idea.

00:50:07.920 --> 00:50:11.920
I was skeptical a few minutes ago, but I do
think there's a nugget of a really big and

00:50:11.920 --> 00:50:13.910
intriguing idea in Paris.

00:50:13.910 --> 00:50:19.079
What you basically did was had countries show
up and say, here's our expression of our commitment,

00:50:19.079 --> 00:50:22.170
what we used to call in the foreign aid business
"best efforts."

00:50:22.170 --> 00:50:24.210
Here's what we're going to commit to doing.

00:50:24.210 --> 00:50:29.200
It's not legally binding, but we're, in many
cases, going to state it: by so and so date,

00:50:29.200 --> 00:50:32.900
we're going to try to peak our output at this
amount.

00:50:32.900 --> 00:50:35.880
Some countries would simply say, like China,
by 2030, we're going to peak; we're not going

00:50:35.880 --> 00:50:37.730
to tell you what it is.

00:50:37.730 --> 00:50:39.089
India wouldn't even go that far.

00:50:39.089 --> 00:50:43.220
But many countries did say, here's what we
aim to do.

00:50:43.220 --> 00:50:47.820
This, to me, is potentially a new form of
multilateralism.

00:50:47.820 --> 00:50:53.849
Rather than having binding, legal, top-down
agreements, what you have are essentially

00:50:53.849 --> 00:50:55.920
coordination of national policies.

00:50:55.920 --> 00:50:57.760
I can imagine it in the health realm.

00:50:57.760 --> 00:51:02.000
I can imagine it dealing with-we're already
seeing it-in some ways the Financial Stability

00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:08.410
Board is an example of it in the monetary
and banking regulation realm.

00:51:08.410 --> 00:51:11.460
So it's a less-binding, less-classic form
of multilateralism.

00:51:11.460 --> 00:51:16.040
But what it-where it might make the most sense-and
I think you're onto something, Nan-is in those

00:51:16.040 --> 00:51:23.369
areas of behavior which are fundamentally
domestic, but which have international consequences,

00:51:23.369 --> 00:51:28.750
I think the Paris approach represents an intellectual
breakthrough.

00:51:28.750 --> 00:51:34.350
And I think it's not as ambitious, but it's
a far more doable and realistic approach to

00:51:34.350 --> 00:51:37.450
building international support.

00:51:37.450 --> 00:51:39.220
You don't require consensus.

00:51:39.220 --> 00:51:42.460
What you require is domestic best efforts,
and then it becomes additive.

00:51:42.460 --> 00:51:47.950
So, I actually think Paris is an intriguing
way in certain areas of, you know, that's

00:51:47.950 --> 00:51:53.730
being looked at in this report card to go
about the business of making progress on specific

00:51:53.730 --> 00:51:54.740
issues.

00:51:54.740 --> 00:51:56.780
MABRY: Excellent.

00:51:56.780 --> 00:51:58.870
Yes, right here.

00:51:58.870 --> 00:51:59.940
Sir.

00:51:59.940 --> 00:52:04.710
Q: Ettore Greco, Institute International Affairs
in Italy.

00:52:04.710 --> 00:52:12.700
The report gives a relatively optimistic grade
on the efforts to expand free trade.

00:52:12.700 --> 00:52:20.790
But we are now witnessing a mounting resistance
and opposition, both in U.S. and Europe.

00:52:20.790 --> 00:52:28.589
Perhaps the grade given by the report reflects
the successful negotiations on the TPP.

00:52:28.589 --> 00:52:39.510
But now, for instance, in the U.S., both presumptive
nominees are campaigning against the transatlantic

00:52:39.510 --> 00:52:41.940
pact, the transatlantic treaty.

00:52:41.940 --> 00:52:44.740
So, could you please comment?

00:52:44.740 --> 00:52:50.290
Perhaps this is the type of difference between
shortsighted and longsighted view?

00:52:50.290 --> 00:52:51.290
I don't know.

00:52:51.290 --> 00:52:53.180
So could you please comment on this problem?

00:52:53.180 --> 00:52:55.370
MABRY: So, who'd like to handle that?

00:52:55.370 --> 00:53:01.800
Because you also talk about the TTIP as well
and its possibility of coming or not.

00:53:01.800 --> 00:53:03.990
Professor Lee, would you like to?

00:53:03.990 --> 00:53:05.820
LEE: Yeah, OK.

00:53:05.820 --> 00:53:11.520
If I talk about Asia-Pacific region, you know,
there is a very interesting movement.

00:53:11.520 --> 00:53:17.849
This is because different mode of regional
economic trade organizations or the cooperations.

00:53:17.849 --> 00:53:22.839
Of example, the ASEAN-led 16 countries RCEP,
I think in September they're going to end

00:53:22.839 --> 00:53:27.280
up the 14 rounds of negotiations.

00:53:27.280 --> 00:53:31.800
And then we have also TTP, with 12 signatories,
and-

00:53:31.800 --> 00:53:33.619
MABRY: And South Korea not a part.

00:53:33.619 --> 00:53:36.430
LEE: South Korea like to enter the second
round.

00:53:36.430 --> 00:53:42.540
But with this difficulty of the ratification
in Washington, we'll see.

00:53:42.540 --> 00:53:43.540
(Chuckles.)

00:53:43.540 --> 00:53:44.540
MABRY: (Laughs.)

00:53:44.540 --> 00:53:51.440
LEE: And at the same time, you know, there
is interesting AIIB, the Asia Investment Infrastructure

00:53:51.440 --> 00:53:53.800
Bank, initiated by China.

00:53:53.800 --> 00:54:00.770
So in Asia, we thought there is a coming of
a kind of competition between China-led economic

00:54:00.770 --> 00:54:06.670
and trade or even investment organization
versus U.S.-led TPP.

00:54:06.670 --> 00:54:14.560
But I see this is not a good idea if the economic,
you know, cooperations should be work together.

00:54:14.560 --> 00:54:21.760
So, I think it's more rational for USA to
be a member of AIIB rather than just to, you

00:54:21.760 --> 00:54:23.180
know, vetoing AIIB.

00:54:23.180 --> 00:54:28.320
So, by participating in AIIB, you know, we
can make the governments more democratic and

00:54:28.320 --> 00:54:31.240
transparent rather than by (conflict ?).
HAASS: I'll talk about trade.

00:54:31.240 --> 00:54:35.390
Just on trade very quickly, world trade has
not been growing for about the last five years-very

00:54:35.390 --> 00:54:36.390
little.

00:54:36.390 --> 00:54:39.339
And, you know, more than anything it's because
economies aren't growing.

00:54:39.339 --> 00:54:46.650
But also, it's now been, what-I lost count
of the years, but since, what, 15 years ago,

00:54:46.650 --> 00:54:48.450
the Doha Development Round.

00:54:48.450 --> 00:54:51.480
Global trade talks are going nowhere.

00:54:51.480 --> 00:54:54.859
It's not clear they can revive given, you
know, any time you try to get agreement with

00:54:54.859 --> 00:54:58.800
190 countries, good luck.

00:54:58.800 --> 00:55:01.940
And that's the old-fashioned legally binding
approach, very, very tough.

00:55:01.940 --> 00:55:06.970
So what we're seeing is the proliferation
of regional, and bilateral, and various types

00:55:06.970 --> 00:55:10.590
of multilateral trade agreements, which are
at best second best.

00:55:10.590 --> 00:55:15.450
There's people like Jagdish Bhagwati who think
they're actually quite, on balance, bad.

00:55:15.450 --> 00:55:19.951
But I would simply say I think they're second
best in what issues they can handle, and I

00:55:19.951 --> 00:55:23.900
think it comes up against now also the domestic
pushback.

00:55:23.900 --> 00:55:31.390
So I think it-I think it has been a rough
time for trade.

00:55:31.390 --> 00:55:35.000
And I think more than anything it's-at the
global level you can't get it negotiated,

00:55:35.000 --> 00:55:37.500
and at the domestic level it's not clear you
have the support.

00:55:37.500 --> 00:55:39.119
FULLILOVE: Can I just add one-
MABRY: Sure.

00:55:39.119 --> 00:55:43.380
FULLILOVE: Can I just pick up something Professor
Lee said about the Asian Investment Infrastructure

00:55:43.380 --> 00:55:44.630
Bank?

00:55:44.630 --> 00:55:52.100
Because one issue we haven't-we haven't talked
too much about China so far, and everybody

00:55:52.100 --> 00:55:57.190
in this room knows that one of the features
of international relations is that sometimes

00:55:57.190 --> 00:56:01.099
established and rising powers collide.

00:56:01.099 --> 00:56:05.430
And this relationship between China and the
United States is going to be very important

00:56:05.430 --> 00:56:10.060
to many of the global governance challenges
that we talk about in the report, not just

00:56:10.060 --> 00:56:13.210
the ones in Asia but globally.

00:56:13.210 --> 00:56:18.920
And it seems to me that the United States
and like-minded countries need to get the

00:56:18.920 --> 00:56:26.630
right balance of engaging and hedging with
China, and sometimes we get that balance quite

00:56:26.630 --> 00:56:28.339
wrong.

00:56:28.339 --> 00:56:34.609
For example, on the AIIB, I didn't understand
why the United States was so negative about

00:56:34.609 --> 00:56:37.520
the-about that particular initiative.

00:56:37.520 --> 00:56:43.990
Because if we're not going to allow China
the ability to set up some kind of infrastructure

00:56:43.990 --> 00:56:48.800
investment bank in Asia, where it's required,
then what prerogatives are we going to allow

00:56:48.800 --> 00:56:50.030
China?

00:56:50.030 --> 00:56:55.901
On the other hand, I would be-so I think,
in a way, we were-I think the United States

00:56:55.901 --> 00:56:58.569
was hedging too much in relation to China.

00:56:58.569 --> 00:57:03.030
But then on other issues-and we've mentioned
the South China Sea-I would say that President

00:57:03.030 --> 00:57:04.730
Obama has been too soft.

00:57:04.730 --> 00:57:08.530
I would say he's been too down the engagement
end.

00:57:08.530 --> 00:57:12.660
So this is going to be, this relationship-these
are just examples of this, but this relationship

00:57:12.660 --> 00:57:16.329
between the United States and China is going
to be so important.

00:57:16.329 --> 00:57:24.740
Can the two countries come up-arrive at an
order, a new-a new modus operandi where each

00:57:24.740 --> 00:57:33.579
of them can achieve their objectives, but
allow the other side to exercise its prerogatives

00:57:33.579 --> 00:57:38.020
and allow everybody else in Asia to also exercise
their prerogatives?

00:57:38.020 --> 00:57:39.609
Can they get that balance right?

00:57:39.609 --> 00:57:46.910
And the importance of statecraft, and leadership,
and getting the right mix of engaging and

00:57:46.910 --> 00:57:51.560
hedging, and choosing where to engage and
where to hedge is so hard, which does come

00:57:51.560 --> 00:57:52.560
back to leadership.

00:57:52.560 --> 00:57:53.560
MABRY: Mmm hmm.

00:57:53.560 --> 00:57:54.560
Excellent.

00:57:54.560 --> 00:57:55.900
At the very back there.

00:57:55.900 --> 00:57:59.710
Q: Thank you.

00:57:59.710 --> 00:58:03.180
Hi, my name is Venk Lal.

00:58:03.180 --> 00:58:04.260
I'm with EverKey Global.

00:58:04.260 --> 00:58:06.839
We're part of Wells Fargo Asset Management.

00:58:06.839 --> 00:58:09.460
Thank you very much for all the observations
thus far.

00:58:09.460 --> 00:58:13.569
My question relates to some of the recent
commentary you've all been making about trade

00:58:13.569 --> 00:58:15.520
and coordination.

00:58:15.520 --> 00:58:20.329
If we step back even farther and look at broader
capital flows around the world, and we look

00:58:20.329 --> 00:58:25.980
at all the volatility-particularly in currency
markets which really demonstrate, some would

00:58:25.980 --> 00:58:31.300
argue, diverging economic interests between
different parts of the world-how does the

00:58:31.300 --> 00:58:36.910
Council view prospects for coordination, whether
it's in the reform of Bretton Woods, as there

00:58:36.910 --> 00:58:41.840
was some mention of in this report, or other
policies that could very well take place?

00:58:41.840 --> 00:58:46.099
Or, alternatively, is it just a matter of
two or three or four central banks at a time

00:58:46.099 --> 00:58:48.830
coordinating policy and sort of running away
with the show?

00:58:48.830 --> 00:58:49.830
MABRY: Thank you.

00:58:49.830 --> 00:58:50.850
Want to take that?

00:58:50.850 --> 00:59:00.800
HAASS: Well, I don't think you're going to
get, you know, formal or collective reform

00:59:00.800 --> 00:59:05.760
of the Bretton Woods institutions, particularly
the IMF, which would be the most relevant

00:59:05.760 --> 00:59:06.760
one.

00:59:06.760 --> 00:59:10.599
I think in some ways-I think it was Tim Geithner
who called the rise of the FSB-the Financial

00:59:10.599 --> 00:59:15.190
Stability Board-sort of the fourth pillar
now joining the original three.

00:59:15.190 --> 00:59:25.470
And when-I think if you look at informal central
bank consultations, you look at essentially

00:59:25.470 --> 00:59:30.150
the de facto norms and rules which argue against
certain types of government or central bank

00:59:30.150 --> 00:59:33.310
intervention, there's been a bit of progress.

00:59:33.310 --> 00:59:38.920
I think there's tension between what you need
to do domestically and its consequences internationally.

00:59:38.920 --> 00:59:41.340
That's true for everybody; it's particularly
true for us, since we happen to operating,

00:59:41.340 --> 00:59:44.250
the last time I checked, the reserve currency.

00:59:44.250 --> 00:59:48.890
So we do quantitative easing and we set rates
based upon domestic projections.

00:59:48.890 --> 00:59:54.260
Obviously, though, they have tremendous consequences
for the role of the dollar internationally.

00:59:54.260 --> 00:59:56.080
And there's a-there is a tension there.

00:59:56.080 --> 01:00:00.790
I'm not sure you can solve it though institutional
fixes, but it seems to me one of the things

01:00:00.790 --> 01:00:05.640
that we've got to be sensitive to the consequences,
and you have to have all sorts of formal and

01:00:05.640 --> 01:00:07.020
informal conversations.

01:00:07.020 --> 01:00:09.570
And increasingly, those are taking place.

01:00:09.570 --> 01:00:13.220
I think one of them, I mean, Dan Drezner and
others have written about it, but if you look

01:00:13.220 --> 01:00:19.750
at how the world has evolved, say, since 2008-2009,
I think there's been probably more progress.

01:00:19.750 --> 01:00:25.359
If I were going to grade-put this on the report
card, there's arguably been as much actual

01:00:25.359 --> 01:00:32.119
progress in dealing with the issues you're
raising-the monetary arrangements, banking,

01:00:32.119 --> 01:00:41.579
questions of domestic regulatory reform, increasing-reducing
the gaps between countries and increasing

01:00:41.579 --> 01:00:45.820
the de facto or informal coordination-I would
think there's probably been as much progress

01:00:45.820 --> 01:00:50.710
there as there been in any other-any other
area of, quote unquote, "global governance,"

01:00:50.710 --> 01:00:54.050
which is not to say it's perfect, but I do
think there's been some meaningful progress.

01:00:54.050 --> 01:00:55.050
MABRY: Professor Lee?

01:00:55.050 --> 01:00:56.359
LEE: Can I add?

01:00:56.359 --> 01:01:03.380
Because there is a regional effort, as you
remember, for the 1997-1998 Asian financial

01:01:03.380 --> 01:01:04.380
crisis.

01:01:04.380 --> 01:01:10.790
Many Asian member countries think they need
a kind of regional cooperations in addition

01:01:10.790 --> 01:01:11.790
to IMF.

01:01:11.790 --> 01:01:16.720
So at the time, you know, Japan initiated
Asian Monetary Fund, but it was opposed by

01:01:16.720 --> 01:01:17.920
Washington.

01:01:17.920 --> 01:01:24.280
So ever since then, at least with the Chiang
Mai Initiative, at least we set up the kind

01:01:24.280 --> 01:01:26.770
of currency swap mechanism.

01:01:26.770 --> 01:01:30.190
Now-used to be a bilateral, but now multilateral-like.

01:01:30.190 --> 01:01:32.829
So, there is a kind of currency swapping.

01:01:32.829 --> 01:01:37.079
You know, when you have a liquidity problem,
we can lend each other.

01:01:37.079 --> 01:01:44.530
So, that's kind of progress to-from the region
to reinforce some weakness that the global

01:01:44.530 --> 01:01:51.610
organizations such as IMF cannot provide,
and at the right time.

01:01:51.610 --> 01:01:52.610
MABRY: OK.

01:01:52.610 --> 01:01:53.610
We're going to go right here, and then we'll
go back there.

01:01:53.610 --> 01:01:54.610
Q: Rita Hauser.

01:01:54.610 --> 01:02:01.430
I find it a little depressing or shocking
that, in your list of items, you have not

01:02:01.430 --> 01:02:07.880
listed the grave humanitarian problems that
are facing the world for last two years: migration,

01:02:07.880 --> 01:02:13.020
displacement being among them, but massive
violations of human rights, all of which the

01:02:13.020 --> 01:02:19.630
entire world community is dedicated to dealing
with and working on a global basis.

01:02:19.630 --> 01:02:24.710
We tend to talk about the refugee crisis,
oh, it's in Syria and Iraq, but it's all over.

01:02:24.710 --> 01:02:28.550
It's in Asia, it's in Latin America, it's
in Australia, it's everywhere.

01:02:28.550 --> 01:02:30.700
Why wasn't it listed, Patrick?

01:02:30.700 --> 01:02:32.750
And what do you guys think about it?

01:02:32.750 --> 01:02:33.750
(Laughter.)

01:02:33.750 --> 01:02:35.660
MABRY: So, we'll go-are you game?

01:02:35.660 --> 01:02:36.660
MR.

01:02:36.660 --> 01:02:37.660
: Let's go to Patrick.

01:02:37.660 --> 01:02:38.660
It's OK.

01:02:38.660 --> 01:02:42.880
HAASS: Stewart, you're-
PATRICK: That's a very good question, Rita.

01:02:42.880 --> 01:02:52.190
We did incorporate it as a spillover consequence
of violent conflict and both interstate, obviously.

01:02:52.190 --> 01:02:55.339
Although people don't pay attention too much
to the displacement in Ukraine, there's been

01:02:55.339 --> 01:03:00.880
over a million and a half people in Ukraine
that have been displaced.

01:03:00.880 --> 01:03:06.550
And obviously most of the folks, although
not all of the folks fleeing into Europe and

01:03:06.550 --> 01:03:13.010
elsewhere, or in the 500,000 in the Dadaab
refugee camp in Kenya, the world's largest

01:03:13.010 --> 01:03:23.160
refugee camp, are obviously victims from displacement
of violent conflict in Somalia, primarily.

01:03:23.160 --> 01:03:27.829
Arguably, it should be added as another category.

01:03:27.829 --> 01:03:32.910
And the-one of the difficulties, of course,
is that there's-there are different categories

01:03:32.910 --> 01:03:37.790
of international threats and problems.

01:03:37.790 --> 01:03:45.260
And parsing between refugees and migrants,
those are both two issues that conceivably

01:03:45.260 --> 01:03:46.829
we could add to this.

01:03:46.829 --> 01:03:50.490
But they increasingly overlap, and it's actually
one of the things that we discussed in one

01:03:50.490 --> 01:03:53.630
of the sessions at our workshop yesterday.

01:03:53.630 --> 01:04:01.380
HAASS: I also think, Rita, in defense of what
we did, you can deal with refugees and people

01:04:01.380 --> 01:04:06.109
on the move either within or across borders
as an issue in and of itself, or you can deal

01:04:06.109 --> 01:04:13.270
with it mainly as a consequence of bad governance
and internal conflict or interstate conflict.

01:04:13.270 --> 01:04:15.349
And this report largely deals with it in the
latter.

01:04:15.349 --> 01:04:21.520
So there's not going to be a solution, ultimately,
to problems of displacement or refugees through

01:04:21.520 --> 01:04:24.110
some kind of a global legal mechanism dealing
with it.

01:04:24.110 --> 01:04:29.000
What we've got to do is deal with the root
causes that's generating the situations where

01:04:29.000 --> 01:04:31.660
these people are forced to flee from their
homes.

01:04:31.660 --> 01:04:36.510
So I think the emphasis in this report is
more on what's leading to it, rather than

01:04:36.510 --> 01:04:37.510
the results of it.

01:04:37.510 --> 01:04:40.130
MABRY: Of course, there are international
institutions and legal institutions.

01:04:40.130 --> 01:04:42.670
There is transnational government supposedly
around human rights.

01:04:42.670 --> 01:04:44.220
HAASS: It's got to be-no-
Q: Yes, there are legal institutions.

01:04:44.220 --> 01:04:49.109
HAASS: I understand that, but all I'm saying
is the principal driver of this is-and the

01:04:49.109 --> 01:04:53.440
way to resolve it is not going to be whatever
comes out of the U.N. this year dealing with

01:04:53.440 --> 01:04:54.440
refugees.

01:04:54.440 --> 01:04:57.780
And "global governance," quote/unquote, is
not going to resolve it if we don't do something

01:04:57.780 --> 01:05:02.720
on the ground in places like Syria, in parts
of Africa, in the Ukraine.

01:05:02.720 --> 01:05:03.930
MABRY: OK.

01:05:03.930 --> 01:05:04.930
Yes?

01:05:04.930 --> 01:05:05.930
Q: Hi.

01:05:05.930 --> 01:05:08.160
Can you hear me?

01:05:08.160 --> 01:05:10.630
Giacomo Landi with DNB Bank.

01:05:10.630 --> 01:05:16.760
I think we got a good idea of the Israeli-Iranian
situation over the next 12, 15 years, and

01:05:16.760 --> 01:05:20.010
maybe some hard lines that Israel has.

01:05:20.010 --> 01:05:26.369
I was struck by the response on North Korea,
and I can appreciate the geography of Seoul.

01:05:26.369 --> 01:05:34.610
But where does South Korea see the North-South
situation developing over the next 10, 15

01:05:34.610 --> 01:05:35.610
years?

01:05:35.610 --> 01:05:39.460
Obviously, extremely harsh rhetoric from the
North directed toward the United States.

01:05:39.460 --> 01:05:41.410
At some point, we may need to act.

01:05:41.410 --> 01:05:46.150
But is there a situation where the South actually
would take the lead on this?

01:05:46.150 --> 01:05:52.599
LEE: Actually, any president of South Korean
government has tried to persuade Pyongyang

01:05:52.599 --> 01:05:59.270
to improve the inter-Korean relations, like
opening up the Kaesong Industrial Complex,

01:05:59.270 --> 01:06:06.020
and then also Kumgang Mountain, many things,
and also tried to set up some economic cooperations.

01:06:06.020 --> 01:06:13.970
But, you know, Pyongyang has responded with
a continuous nuclear threat.

01:06:13.970 --> 01:06:20.380
It was very difficult, for example, the current
government tried to prepare in a unification

01:06:20.380 --> 01:06:27.930
is whole packages of investment, infrastructure,
and trade, and port development, but they

01:06:27.930 --> 01:06:33.400
all stopped after the fourth nuclear test
in this January.

01:06:33.400 --> 01:06:36.680
So even Kaesong Industrial Complex was shut
down.

01:06:36.680 --> 01:06:38.109
Too bad, right?

01:06:38.109 --> 01:06:44.730
So, it's very difficult for Seoul to take
in the driver's seat because if Pyongyang

01:06:44.730 --> 01:06:51.150
ignore Seoul, and they like to talk with Washington
to get Washington's attention to normalize

01:06:51.150 --> 01:06:52.530
the relations, they hope.

01:06:52.530 --> 01:06:59.950
Of course, but Washington will need to - the
Pyongyang's sincere attitude of engagement

01:06:59.950 --> 01:07:02.460
to dismantle the nuclear programs.

01:07:02.460 --> 01:07:07.200
HAASS: I'll just say what worries me-and I
think your question gets at it-is there is

01:07:07.200 --> 01:07:13.960
potentially a significant tension, or gap,
between how Seoul and Washington view North

01:07:13.960 --> 01:07:14.960
Korea.

01:07:14.960 --> 01:07:19.090
If Seoul's priority is at virtually any cost
to avoid another war on the peninsula, which

01:07:19.090 --> 01:07:24.020
is understandable; and if Washington is increasingly
concerned about, among other things, North

01:07:24.020 --> 01:07:28.849
Korea's nuclear reach, whether it goes on
the market or has missiles that can reach

01:07:28.849 --> 01:07:34.890
the United States; you can imagine where suddenly
the coordination of this alliance becomes

01:07:34.890 --> 01:07:37.700
extraordinarily difficult.

01:07:37.700 --> 01:07:42.970
And I think that's quite a possibility over
the next decade, as North Korean nuclear capabilities

01:07:42.970 --> 01:07:43.970
grow.

01:07:43.970 --> 01:07:47.470
At the same time, their conventional capabilities
are also considerable.

01:07:47.470 --> 01:07:53.140
I think they're-the Washington-Seoul bifurcation,
potentially, is a-is a real challenge.

01:07:53.140 --> 01:07:58.300
MABRY: General Yadlin, if I can ask you a
question with regards to that coordination

01:07:58.300 --> 01:08:01.140
idea amongst challenges.

01:08:01.140 --> 01:08:04.700
When it comes to your neighborhood-and especially
I'm thinking of the fight against Islamic

01:08:04.700 --> 01:08:09.270
State-is this going to be-is this an issue
of transnational governance, or is it actually

01:08:09.270 --> 01:08:15.250
an issue of bilateral or even unilateral action
on the part of great powers or regional powers?

01:08:15.250 --> 01:08:22.730
YADLIN: Unlike like the view from Washington,
which look at the Islamic State as the most

01:08:22.730 --> 01:08:33.630
dangerous phenomena in the Middle East, we
still think that the most formidable threat

01:08:33.630 --> 01:08:41.759
to the Middle East-maybe the Saudis are thinking
alike-is the Islamic Republic of Iran.

01:08:41.759 --> 01:08:52.199
And when you look at it from an Israeli perspective,
Daesh is not a threat to Israel today.

01:08:52.199 --> 01:08:59.470
If you put it on a scale of how many tons
of explosive our enemy can launch to Tel Aviv?

01:08:59.470 --> 01:09:05.420
So, the Iranians, hundred of missiles with
heavy warheads.

01:09:05.420 --> 01:09:09.250
Hezbollah, maybe thousands.

01:09:09.250 --> 01:09:12.730
Some from Hamas, not too many.

01:09:12.730 --> 01:09:18.210
Daesh, with all due respect, they are fighting
with Hezbollah as we speak.

01:09:18.210 --> 01:09:24.279
And if I want to be cynical a little bit,
I wish success for both.

01:09:24.279 --> 01:09:25.279
(Laughter.)

01:09:25.279 --> 01:09:27.560
So Daesh is not our main concern.

01:09:27.560 --> 01:09:35.549
It's still Iran that basically the threat
to the Middle East.

01:09:35.549 --> 01:09:42.650
And when you look from perspective in Tel
Aviv, all the world is fighting Daesh.

01:09:42.650 --> 01:09:46.410
Two very respected coalitions.

01:09:46.410 --> 01:09:52.811
One led by the president of the United States,
even though he never called the 65 countries

01:09:52.811 --> 01:09:59.170
to his headquarters to talk to them seriously,
but it's a serious coalition.

01:09:59.170 --> 01:10:05.790
And there is another, more ugly coalition,
led by Russia, and Iran, and Syria, and Hezbollah.

01:10:05.790 --> 01:10:07.790
And all of them fighting ISIS.

01:10:07.790 --> 01:10:11.550
With Iran, unfortunately, we were left alone.

01:10:11.550 --> 01:10:15.070
So we take it much more seriously.

01:10:15.070 --> 01:10:21.430
MABRY: Your other earlier answer about Iran,
and kind of what happens in the 12 to 15 years,

01:10:21.430 --> 01:10:25.280
and the hope, basically, of those who supported
the agreement, hoping that Iran will change-that

01:10:25.280 --> 01:10:30.980
Iran's on a course toward change-reminded
me of kind of the lessons that we also may

01:10:30.980 --> 01:10:35.090
have learned, I don't know, maybe too much,
from the Cold War: the U.S. facing its greatest

01:10:35.090 --> 01:10:38.050
adversary in the Soviet Union.

01:10:38.050 --> 01:10:41.280
There was-there was certainly containment,
and there was certainly military power involved,

01:10:41.280 --> 01:10:47.980
but also there was waiting through a containment
of Russia while Russia crumbled.

01:10:47.980 --> 01:10:53.110
The hope that internal dynamics will mean
that we won't have to act militarily with

01:10:53.110 --> 01:10:56.570
regard to Iran, do you think that's silly,
not possible?

01:10:56.570 --> 01:10:57.570
Is it possible?

01:10:57.570 --> 01:10:58.570
Might it work?

01:10:58.570 --> 01:10:59.719
Might Iran be on the road to change?

01:10:59.719 --> 01:11:03.030
Might there be a way for the international
community to help that along?

01:11:03.030 --> 01:11:11.650
YADLIN: See, the art of using your political
and military instrument is when to use them

01:11:11.650 --> 01:11:13.690
and when not to use them.

01:11:13.690 --> 01:11:20.260
And I think it was already mentioned in this
panel that the U.S. demonstrated the two extreme:

01:11:20.260 --> 01:11:31.170
overreaction-two wars, $2 trillion, a decade
long, not achieving the objectives; and there

01:11:31.170 --> 01:11:39.480
was another demonstration of inaction-the
Syrians crossed the red line, used, by the

01:11:39.480 --> 01:11:49.350
way, if we speak about proliferation, chemical
weapon-a very dangerous chemical weapon, sarin,

01:11:49.350 --> 01:11:52.679
2013, and nothing was done.

01:11:52.679 --> 01:11:58.820
By the way, I don't know if you are aware,
but a month ago they used it again, even though

01:11:58.820 --> 01:12:05.080
there was an agreement, international cooperation
between U.S. and Russia to take the chemical

01:12:05.080 --> 01:12:06.080
weapon from Syria.

01:12:06.080 --> 01:12:11.960
A small stock was left and used last month.

01:12:11.960 --> 01:12:17.620
It was published in Haaretz in Israel by a
very respected reporter.

01:12:17.620 --> 01:12:19.330
Nobody pay attention.

01:12:19.330 --> 01:12:20.500
Nobody pay attention.

01:12:20.500 --> 01:12:22.910
Most of the people didn't want to pay attention.

01:12:22.910 --> 01:12:27.150
So, what are the consequences of using chemical
weapon?

01:12:27.150 --> 01:12:34.630
So, going back to your question about cooperation,
what's really interesting in the Middle East

01:12:34.630 --> 01:12:42.130
is that the interest of all the players are
(interwoven ?). And there is a new term there,

01:12:42.130 --> 01:12:44.210
which called "friendemy."

01:12:44.210 --> 01:12:46.960
It's not "the enemy of my enemy is my friend."

01:12:46.960 --> 01:12:50.820
In the same time that I'm fighting my enemy,
I'm also cooperating with him.

01:12:50.820 --> 01:12:56.400
In the same time that the U.S. and Russia
are fighting ISIS together, they have different

01:12:56.400 --> 01:12:57.400
interests.

01:12:57.400 --> 01:13:03.150
In the same times that Assad fight ISIS, he
is buying oil from ISIS.

01:13:03.150 --> 01:13:10.480
And both of them fight, unfortunately, the
oppositions that is supported by the U.S.

01:13:10.480 --> 01:13:11.590
Our enemy is Hamas.

01:13:11.590 --> 01:13:17.070
Who is giving Hamas materials to rebuild Gaza?

01:13:17.070 --> 01:13:18.230
Is it Saudi Arabia?

01:13:18.230 --> 01:13:19.300
Is it Turkey?

01:13:19.300 --> 01:13:21.590
No, it's Israel.

01:13:21.590 --> 01:13:28.000
And we are playing with the tensions that
we want Gaza to reconstruct and to rebuild,

01:13:28.000 --> 01:13:36.380
but we know that a high percentage is going
to build an attack tunnels for the terrorists.

01:13:36.380 --> 01:13:38.800
So it's a complicated area.

01:13:38.800 --> 01:13:44.920
There is a lot of convergence of interest
to Israel with countries that in the past

01:13:44.920 --> 01:13:47.960
we didn't see as friends.

01:13:47.960 --> 01:13:56.420
Because when I meet some of my friends from
Saudi Arabia-not officially, not on the record-the

01:13:56.420 --> 01:14:03.360
basically-the basic argument is, look, on
Iran, we are on the same page.

01:14:03.360 --> 01:14:05.930
Unfortunately, the Americans are here.

01:14:05.930 --> 01:14:09.130
On Egypt, we are on the same page: we both
support Sisi.

01:14:09.130 --> 01:14:14.580
Unfortunately, we are not sure that the Americans
are not supporting Muslim Brotherhood.

01:14:14.580 --> 01:14:22.010
On Assad, they know that I personally think
that he is a war criminal and should be removed,

01:14:22.010 --> 01:14:24.530
so we are on the same page.

01:14:24.530 --> 01:14:29.250
Unfortunately, on the Palestinian-Israeli
issue, we are not on the same page.

01:14:29.250 --> 01:14:35.670
So my Saudi friend will say, only solve the
Israeli-Palestinian issue and we will be brothers

01:14:35.670 --> 01:14:38.770
in the open.

01:14:38.770 --> 01:14:45.530
So there are some new opportunities here for
Israel, because when you look at the region,

01:14:45.530 --> 01:14:53.530
we have a lot of interests that converge with
the pragmatic Sunni Arab countries which are

01:14:53.530 --> 01:14:55.210
the majority in the Middle East.

01:14:55.210 --> 01:14:58.630
MABRY: That is going to have to be our last
word.

01:14:58.630 --> 01:15:00.090
Please join me in thanking the panel.

01:15:00.090 --> 01:15:01.090
And please look at the report.

01:15:01.090 --> 01:15:02.090
(Applause.)

01:15:02.090 --> 01:15:03.150
I'm sorry, I'll just say one more thing before
I go.

01:15:03.150 --> 01:15:07.260
Look at-look at the report, because it does
lay out prescriptions of how we can change

01:15:07.260 --> 01:15:12.380
what, as Stewart said at the beginning, is
a creaky international institution system

01:15:12.380 --> 01:15:15.969
to help us further along the road toward progress
on all these issues.

01:15:15.969 --> 01:15:18.679
Thank you.

01:15:18.679 --> 01:15:21.390
(Applause.)

01:15:21.390 --> 01:15:32.210
FULLILOVE: Thank you, Marcus.

01:15:32.210 --> 01:15:34.920
(Applause.)

01:15:34.920 --> 01:15:37.620
(END)

